# DocGen — Sitagliptin Validation Fixes (RS + EI + DEG/EG) + Grounding + Claude Config

**Global rules:** product-agnostic; never hardcode a product, material, code, ppm, or limit; read values only from the uploaded source; the regulatory limit belongs ONLY in the final comparison row, never in a per-material content cell; warn-first, never hard-block; deterministic output. Each ticket explains WHY so the fix targets the root cause, not the symptom.

---

## PART A — RESIDUAL SOLVENT (RS) BUGS

### RS-1. Wrong column read: Option-1 Limit used instead of vendor "Maximum Expected Amount" (headline math bug)
**What happens:** For the Opadry coating, every solvent exposure is inflated. The Opadry PRDS prints TWO labelled numeric columns — **"Option 1 Limit"** and **"Maximum Expected Amount in Finished Colorcon Product."** The extractor picked the Limit column:

| Solvent | PRDS Max Expected (correct content) | PRDS Option-1 Limit | Doc used |
|---|---|---|---|
| Ethylene glycol | 101 ppm | 620 | **620** ❌ |
| Methanol | 2000 | 3000 | **3000** ❌ |
| 1,4-Dioxane | 1 | 380 | **380** ❌ |
| Methyl acetate | 2000 | 5000 | **5000** ❌ |

**Why:** the RS annexure classifier (`_classify_solvent_ppm` / `process_rs_annexure`) treats an "Option 1 Limit" number as the material's `converted_ppm`. In a USP <467> assessment the per-material content must be the **expected/measured amount**, and the Option-1 limit is the regulatory ceiling that goes ONLY in the "Residual solvent Limit as per current USP <467>" comparison row. This is the SAME wrong-column class as the EI Aerosil bug.
**Fix:** when a solvent statement carries BOTH a limit column ("Option 1 Limit", "USP limit", "ICH limit", NMT) AND an expected/measured/typical content column ("Maximum Expected Amount", "Typical", "Result", "Content"), set `converted_ppm` = the CONTENT value, and record the limit separately as `spec_limit_ppm` for the comparison row only. Never let the Option-1/regulatory limit become the material content. Detect the two-column layout by header semantics, product-agnostic. Expected: Opadry EG=101, Methanol=2000, 1,4-Dioxane=1, Methyl acetate=2000; recompute exposures (EG 12×101/1e6=0.00121; 1,4-Dioxane 12×1/1e6=0.000012).

### RS-2. Regulatory limits leaking into ingredient CONTENT cells (fabricated exposures)
**What happens:** The Sitagliptin API row and Propyl gallate row show content values equal to the ICH Q3C Option-1 limits (Benzene 2, Toluene 890, Methanol 3000, Ethyl acetate/IPA/Ethanol 5000, Triethanolamine 1500, 1-Propanol 5000). A vendor CoA cannot plausibly contain the entire limit table as its content — these are limits mis-read as content.
**Why:** the `excel_limit` / annexure-limit path is falling back to the USP master limit when the vendor annexure has no per-solvent numeric content (the code comment explicitly forbids this: *"must NEVER fall back to the USP <467> master regulatory limit … it belongs solely in the final comparison row"*). The guard is being bypassed for these rows.
**Fix:** an ingredient content cell may be populated ONLY from (a) the vendor annexure's expected/measured content, or (b) a user override. If neither exists → render **NA**, never the master limit. Audit every path that can write `converted_ppm`/limit into a per-component cell and ensure the USP master limit is unreachable there. Flag the API row for source verification (the Jiuzhou declaration was not machine-readable, so confirm its true content).

### RS-3. Annexure material-name extraction defaults to the API/product name
**What happens:** Section 9 annexure list is corrupted — three annexures are labelled "Sitagliptin phosphate monohydrate":
- Annexure 4 → actually the **CEOLUS MCC** PRDS (Asahi Kasei)
- Annexures 7 & 8 → actually **Opadry** PRDS (Colorcon)
**Why:** when `process_rs_annexure` / `rs_strip_descriptor` cannot extract a material name from the document body/title (these PRDS put the product name in an image or an awkward header), it is falling back to the request's product name ("Sitagliptin") instead of the vendor/filename-derived name. Same class as the DEG/EG "raw filename as material name" miss.
**Fix:** the material-name fallback ladder must be body → candidates → document title → **filename-derived name** → "Material [N]" — and must NEVER fall back to the product/API name. When the body yields nothing, use `_rs_filename_derived_name` (CEOLUS…, PRDS 85F565140… → the coating/MCC identity) so the annexure list shows the real vendor material.

### RS-4. Supplier-declaration cross-reference collapses to the last annexure number
**What happens:** Table 3 shows EVERY ingredient → "Annexure 10", while Section 9 correctly numbers them 1–10.
**Why:** in `build_rs_payload`, `orig_to_display` / the supplier-declaration loop is resolving each ingredient's annexure reference to the last assigned display number instead of the annexure actually mapped to that ingredient (a dict overwrite or a single shared variable).
**Fix:** resolve each ingredient's supplier-table reference from ITS mapped annexure's display number, per row. Table 3 refs must match Section 9 (Calcium Phosphate→Annexure 1, MCC→3, Aerosil→5, Opadry Brown→6, etc.).

### RS-5. Multi-material annexure not mapped to all its materials (Croscarmellose unmatched)
**What happens:** Croscarmellose renders "-" (unmatched), yet the IFF Cork PRDS is a single document covering BOTH Avicel MCC AND Ac-Di-Sol Croscarmellose (§4.36.2 gives Croscarmellose's RS statement: Ethanol <100 ppm).
**Why:** annexure→material mapping is one-to-one; the IFF PRDS mapped to MCC only, leaving Croscarmellose with no annexure. IDENTICAL to the EI Croscarmellose miss on the same document.
**Fix (shared with EI):** make annexure→material mapping many-to-many. When one annexure names multiple product families that match multiple composition rows, bind it to EACH (MCC AND Croscarmellose). Croscarmellose then takes the IFF Ac-Di-Sol RS statement instead of unmatched.

### RS-6. Coating-variant-per-strength: wrong Opadry variants in the 100 mg tables
**What happens:** the assessment is for the 100 mg strength (Opadry **Brown**, 12 mg). But Opadry **Yellow** (the 50 mg variant) appears as a row with "q.s.", and Opadry **Orange** (25 mg) is dropped.
**Why:** the composition carries three colour variants, one per strength (Brown→100, Yellow→50, Orange→25). The single-strength parser selected the 100 mg column but did not filter out the variants whose quantity is NA at that strength, and rendered the NA-quantity Yellow row as q.s.
**Fix:** for the selected strength, a coating variant whose quantity is NA/blank at that strength is NOT part of the formulation — exclude it entirely (do not render it, and never as q.s.). Only Brown (12 mg) belongs in the 100 mg tables.

### RS-minor
- Table numbering collides: assessment tables run to "Table 10", then the risk-summary is also "Table 7" — renumber sequentially.
- Preserve ingredient-name casing ("Magnesium stearate", not "magnesium stearate").
- **Correctly handled (do not regress):** Peter Greven mag-stearate negative declaration → all NA/0; Asahi CEOLUS negative declaration → NA; merged mag stearate Part-1+II = 10 mg; DP mass 412; ×1.0 multiplier.

